Messages.Body:
aa...@cats.ucsc.edu (Thomas R Wylie) writes...
}James R. McClure Jr. <jrmc...@ulkyvm.louisville.edu> wrote:
}>Ok, if we agree that this is totally a marketting move (an I think we do),
}>then why doesn't WotC take the simpler win-win solution I proposed yesterday:
}>leave the card backs alone, but change the deck, pack, and stand displays to
}>read "Vampire: the Eternal Struggle." This way, they get the increased market
}>penetration they are looking for AND the old and new cards are fully
}>compatible. I don't see why WotC doesn't choose this alternative.
}
}Because it will look stupid and inconsistent to new players for these holdouts
}from the old game to be lying around. The name of the game is changing,
}so the presentation of the game is being changed to reflect this. Maybe
}people who play Jyhad don't think it would look stupid for the V:TES cards
}to say Jyhad on the backs, but we disagree.
}
}Tom Wylie rec.games.trading-cards.* Network Representative for
}aa...@cats.ucsc.edu Wizards of the Coast, Inc.

Which doesn't answer the question of why didn't they just reaname the bloody
thing "Vampire: The Eternal Jyhad" and leave the card backs as the were?

This would have made everybody happy. Card backs stay the same = players
happy. "Vampire" plastered all over the box and displays = trendy vampire
craze "this has something to do with vampires" recognition factor = WotC
happy. But no, they have to go and do things the hard way. So much for
cleverness.

--- Carl
